(6047014, 886745, 38162, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by rhadshaw:</b></div><hr/><i> I like the viewpoint on this shot; it provides an interesting perspective. However, there is a bit of a colour cast from the florescent lighting (a yellow/green cast in the highlights in the floor and on the seat sides) that detracts from the image in my eyes. Have you tried a black and white conversion on this image? </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>its called toning with gradients.  look it up.</td>', 'Originally posted by rhadshaw: I like the viewpoint on this shot; it provides an interesting perspective. However, there is a bit of a colour cast from the florescent lighting (a yellow/green cast in the highlights in the floor and on the seat sides) that detracts from the image in my eyes. Have you tried a black and white conversion on this image?   its called toning with gradients.  look it up.', '2010-06-08 19:50:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(6047018, 886745, 38162, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by cowtownmom:</b></div><hr/><i> Don''t know if you meant to have a bit of a tilt to the picture or not.  It doesn''t bother me because I like the perspective but it might others. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>look to the far end of the image and you will see that this was rotated on purpose so that the horizon line is perfect.<br/></td>', 'Originally posted by cowtownmom: Don''t know if you meant to have a bit of a tilt to the picture or not.  It doesn''t bother me because I like the perspective but it might others.   look to the far end of the image and you will see that this was rotated on purpose so that the horizon line is perfect.', '2010-06-08 19:51:20.000000', NULL, 0),
